---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import EventStream from '../../../components/EventStream.astro';
---
<DocsLayout title="Session discipline" pathname="/docs/features/discipline" section="Features">
  <h1>Session discipline</h1>
  <p class="dx-lede">
    Hooks register a session the moment it starts and checkpoint governance before every context compaction — so you never open a session the spine didn't witness.
  </p>

  <p>
    Discipline you have to remember isn't discipline. A session that forgets to register leaves no record of what it touched, and a context compaction can drop the last un-recorded work silently — you only notice when the trail is already gone. Máddu wires that discipline into Claude Code hooks so it happens without you.
  </p>

  <div class="dx-code">
    <div class="dx-code-head"><span class="dx-code-dot"></span> terminal</div>
    <pre><code>$ <span class="tok-k">maddu hooks install</span>
<span class="tok-c">wired SessionStart · SessionEnd · PreCompact into .claude/settings.json</span></code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <div class="dx-cards">
    <div class="dx-card">
      <span class="dx-card-title"><span>Start records itself</span></span>
      <p><code>SessionStart</code> auto-registers a session and writes it to the spine; <code>SessionEnd</code> closes it. No manual <code>--session</code> flag to forget — the record exists before you type the first prompt.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="dx-card">
      <span class="dx-card-title"><span>Compaction leaves a mark</span></span>
      <p><code>PreCompact</code> writes a <code>COMPACTION_CHECKPOINT</code> before every compaction — manual <code>/compact</code> and automatic alike — capturing the last <code>slice-stop</code> as the durable anchor: anything after it that wasn't recorded did not survive.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="dx-card">
      <span class="dx-card-title"><span>Stale rituals block the edit</span></span>
      <p>Since v1.97.0, <code>PreToolUse</code> denies a mutating edit when a ritual is stale (no session, lane, governing goal/plan, an overdue <code>slice-stop</code>, or an uncommitted pileup), naming the first blocker and its remedy. Tier-scaled: <code>strict</code> blocks, <code>standard</code> is graduated, <code>relaxed</code> nudges.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="dx-card">
      <span class="dx-card-title"><span>It never breaks you</span></span>
      <p>The hooks never exit <code>2</code>. A governance instrument must never crash the session it observes, so any evaluator/git/parse error degrades to allow — enforcement can only deny an edit through a structured decision. Reads and the remedy commands are never gated, so a block can't deadlock its own fix.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="dx-note">
    <span class="dx-note-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◆</span>
    <p><strong>Block-and-tell, never auto-perform.</strong> Máddu never auto-slice-stops or auto-commits to clear a block — that would fake the record. It stops the edit and hands you the one command that fixes it. At enforcing tiers, <code>goal done</code>/<code>plan complete</code> also refuse to close while a required gate is red; the <code>discipline-observed</code> doctor gate flags a pinned tier with no hook wired; <code>maddu hooks uninstall</code> is the off-switch.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="dx-note">
    <span class="dx-note-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◆</span>
    <p><strong>The checkpoint knows what's at risk.</strong> Each checkpoint records the trigger, the last recorded <code>slice-stop</code>, handoff currency, open approvals, and active claims. <code>maddu orient</code> auto-announces the latest one on session start, and doctor validates that the hook stanza is still current.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>On the record</h2>
  <p>A disciplined session is a clean arc on the spine — start, a stopped slice, a checkpoint, close:</p>

  <EventStream
    eyebrow="Session start to close, checkpoint in between"
    source=".maddu/events/2026-07-05.ndjson"
    entries={[
      { time: '08:31:04.212', type: 'SESSION_REGISTERED',      tone: 'session',  payload: 'role=implementer · trigger=SessionStart',              actor: 'claude-code' },
      { time: '09:02:47.883', type: 'SLICE_STOPPED',           tone: 'approval', payload: 'targets=discipline.astro · gates=7 pass',              actor: 'claude-code' },
      { time: '09:18:22.109', type: 'COMPACTION_CHECKPOINT',   tone: 'accent',   payload: 'trigger=auto · anchor=SLICE_STOPPED@09:02 · approvals=0', actor: 'claude-code' },
      { time: '09:44:51.640', type: 'SESSION_CLOSED',          tone: 'session',  payload: 'trigger=SessionEnd · duration=73m',                    actor: 'claude-code' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Related verbs: <a href="/capabilities/hooks"><code>maddu hooks</code></a>, <a href="/capabilities/agents"><code>maddu agents</code></a>, <a href="/capabilities/orient"><code>maddu orient</code></a>, and <a href="/capabilities/session"><code>maddu session</code></a>. The companion verb <code>maddu agents</code> registers a self-contained "install maddu" stanza into your global agent instruction files — Claude, Codex, Gemini, or a custom path — so the framework is reachable by natural language from any future repo.
  </p>

  <div class="dx-pager">
    <a class="dx-pager-link" href="/docs/features">
      <span class="dx-pager-dir">&larr; Prev</span>
      <span class="dx-pager-title">Features</span>
    </a>
    <a class="dx-pager-link is-next" href="/docs/features/autonomy">
      <span class="dx-pager-dir">Next &rarr;</span>
      <span class="dx-pager-title">Earned autonomy</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
